::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which model represents 1000 dollars growing by 5% annually? {
= A(t)=1000(1.05)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 1000 and 5% growth gives factor 1.05.
~ A(t)=1000(0.95)<sup>t</sup>#This models 5% decay.
~ A(t)=1.05(1000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
~ A(t)=1000+5t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>2</sub>(64)? {
~ 4#2 to the fourth is 16.
= 6#Correct. 2 raised to the sixth power is 64.
~ 8#2 to the eighth is 256.
~ 32#This is not the needed exponent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>3</sub>(xy<sup>2</sup>). {
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x+y<sup>2</sup>)#This changes multiplication to addition inside the argument.
~ 2log<sub>3</sub>(xy)#The exponent applies only to y.
= log<sub>3</sub>(x)+2log<sub>3</sub>(y)#Correct. Use product and power properties.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x)-2log<sub>3</sub>(y)#Subtraction would represent division.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Solve log<sub>5</sub>(x)=3. {
~ x=15#This multiplies base and output.
~ x=8#This adds base and output.
~ x=2#This subtracts incorrectly.
= x=125#Correct. Rewrite as x=5<sup>3</sup>.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 4<sup>x</sup>=30 using logarithms. {
= x=log(30)/log(4)#Correct. Change of base isolates the exponent.
~ x=log(4)/log(30)#This reverses the ratio.
~ x=30/4#This treats the equation as linear.
~ x=120#This multiplies base and output.
}

